## Automatic planning at implementation MR creation

When a predecessor implementation MR first opens against `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`,
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} verifies its source/target/candidate and automatically starts
provisional planning for the earliest eligible task with an existing ready Goal/Spec.
Draft implementation MRs qualify; planning-only/placeholder/probe MRs do not.
Do not wait for CI/Duo, ready status, merge or final acceptance to begin planning.
Reuse the existing planner, honor owner stops and record the MR baseline and pending
gates. Reconcile relevant revisions and final accepted inputs before execution;
closure without integration requires reassessment. Integration is a fallback
checkpoint if initial discovery was missed, never a duplicate dispatch.
Inspection/Plan artifacts only; no code/tests/builds/shared-stack use or early
execution. Final dependency ACCEPTs, Plan review, bindings, budgets, owner decisions
and resource/release gates remain. See [planning policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-planning-after-integration).

## Automatic reassessment and execution release

After final predecessor ACCEPT and required integration CI disposition,
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} automatically reassesses the provisional Plan against the
accepted integration and current Goal/Spec. Material changes need bounded
{{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} Plan updates/review; irrelevant deltas need reconciliation.
When all dependencies, Plan ACCEPT (or explicit direct-tier authority), current
binding, owner decisions, budget and resources are ready, record/relay execution
release and start scoped work without another routine owner-proceed prompt.
Plan existence/CI alone is insufficient. Preserve specific holds, native
permissions and delivery barriers; no budget reset or duplicate qualification.
See [release policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-reassessment-and-execution-release).

## Supervisor progress publication

Supervisor auto-publication: `{{SUPERVISOR_AUTO_PUBLISH}}` (enabled by default). When enabled, {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}},
after completing a PROGRESS update, commits and pushes that update and its associated management records (imported
Results and status, reviews, decisions) to `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` from the main integration checkout without another
owner confirmation. It stages exact owned paths, excludes secrets and machine-local logs, fetches and reconciles
first, never force-pushes, and reports a conflict, protected-branch rule or permission gate instead of bypassing it.
Publication is not review or ACCEPT. {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} delivers Results and status and keeps its task-branch and
merge-request workflow; this grants no direct implementer push. The owner may disable it (`false`) for the repository
or for an explicit task or session; record the override. It is an agent workflow, not a watcher, hook or scheduler.

## Supervisor checkout, delegated review and relay

{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} normally works in the main integration checkout. Prefer bounded subagents for Spec
drafting/research and formal Plan/Result reviews; reviewers are read-only by default, and the supervisor owns the
disposition. When an existing handoff or status check shows a review-ready Plan or Result, start the delegated
review without another owner prompt: verify the exact artifact and phase, skip a duplicate review of unchanged
bytes, and review directly (recording why) when delegation is unavailable. Record the outcome, then relay it with
the remaining gates to the verified coordinator and verify receipt and acknowledgment. The owner may interrupt or
redirect; mandatory escalations and owner merge authority remain. No watcher, transport or early implementation
authority is added. For a bounded delegated review, keep the supervisor turn open with the harness's asynchronous
agent wait/notification interface (the owner may steer meanwhile). Finalize, record and relay the decision before the
final reply. Use bounded waits without repeated inference-based polling; waiting itself uses no model tokens, but
reviewer work and result processing do. An ended turn may not wake automatically, so say so rather than promising it.
Interrupting the supervisor's turn alone is not stop-all: on resuming, inspect child status, keep completed findings
and authorized running work, and do not cancel or re-dispatch automatically. An explicit stop-all or a conflicting
scope, security or owner direction governs; never assume a client cancellation reached child agents.

## Coordinator and workers: naming, dispatch and relocation gate

1. `0-agent-<id>` is reserved for the single active persistent coordinator; its existing agent ID is kept when
   available, and no worker uses the `0-` prefix.
2. Exactly one coordinator is active, verified by complete discovery and the owner-selected pin
   (`pinned-live-verified`). Unknown or ambiguous discovery never justifies another coordinator.
3. A directory name grants no authority, identity or routing; discovery, the verified pin and recorded task
   ownership do.
4. Prefer `<task-id>-agent-<agent-id>-<role>` when supported (for example `g002-agent-<id>-impl`, `-plan`, `-check`).
   {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} workers live under `.claude/worktrees/`; {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} workers that need isolation
   live under `.codex/worktrees/`. Read-only reviewers need no checkout.
5. Before dispatch, record task/role and preferred path if supported; otherwise use native isolated-worker creation.
6. Before task writes, verify actual Git path, branch, HEAD, agent/session owner and task/role. Wrong ownership,
   branch or unsafe isolation fails; a generated basename alone does not.
7. Treat unsupported Claude task/role naming as a harness bug/limitation and continue with its native `agent-<id>`
   worktree, recording the actual task mapping. No task-specific exception or naming approval/prompt is required.
   Never delay authorized work, duplicate sessions or bypass native permissions merely to enforce a name.
8. Relocation defers by default: no move beneath a live process, and no restart or duplicate session for naming. A
   move needs complete discovery, positively verified stopped ownership, separately proven absence of cwd and
   open-file users, a quiet state and a recorded supported same-UUID restart.
9. Only then: `git worktree move`, verify, restart the same UUID, reconcile path and pin provenance. Any failure
   stops.

Owner attribution: relay an actual owner decision as "The owner explicitly authorized this action; {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} is
relaying that authorization", naming the action and decision record. A relayed authorization never overrides a native
permission rejection or a gate that needs direct owner action; report it instead. Observer notices carry no authority.

Details and the full preconditions: [coordination](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md).
Session discovery, routing, receipts and closeout archiving use
[claude-session-handoff](.agents/skills/claude-session-handoff/SKILL.md) and the local
[verification record](docs/agents/CLAUDE-SESSION-HANDOFF.md); supervision resumes with
[repository-supervisor](.agents/skills/repository-supervisor/SKILL.md).

## Combining management and foundation skills

When used with `saas-foundation` or a similar renderer, `sdlc-management` supersedes only overlapping management
process. Renderer dry runs, resolved-input review, informed authorization, conflict safeguards, technical checks and
canonical decisions stay; foundation stages become Goal and acceptance inputs; the renderer's plan is not an
Implementation Plan; unresolved technical conflicts escalate.

## Execution policy

The supervisor states the risk tier (direct, ordinary, or security/data/architecture) in each Goal. After Plan
approval, or within an explicitly authorized direct-tier Goal/Spec without a separate Plan, the implementer works
autonomously within scope; agents never select a lighter tier themselves. Stop and escalate: architecture contradictions, scope
expansion, security-model changes, destructive migrations, public contract changes, new major infrastructure or
vendors, irreversible data-model decisions.

Budgets follow the [budget policy](management/BUDGET_POLICY.md); the default class is {{DEFAULT_BUDGET}}. Effort
recommendations by role: coordinator {{COORDINATOR_EFFORT}} for orchestration, status and handoffs; planning workers
(and a coordinator that plans) {{PLANNING_EFFORT}}; implementation workers {{IMPLEMENTATION_EFFORT}}. Set worker
effort explicitly through supported controls; verify actual or inherited values and record unverifiable effort as
unknown. The owner may raise complex debugging or security work to xhigh.

## Milestone observer activation

Observer auto-activation: `{{OBSERVER_AUTO_ACTIVATE}}` (enabled by default; independent of supervisor publication).
When enabled, once the coordinator pin is verified and the existing supervisor UUID is explicitly bound, the
coordinator workflow starts the companion observer (`watch.py bind`, then `start`, 30-second default) without
another enablement question. Missing, ambiguous, unverified or retired bindings leave it inactive and reported.
There is no name guessing, session creation or permission change. A repeated start is idempotent, and `false`
opts out. Notices, including the transcript-size advisory, carry no authority.

## Validation and integration

Before each batch, fetch and assess the exact delta for validation and execution-authority impact. If irrelevant
to both, record the target range/candidate/reason and defer rebase/input reconstruction. Otherwise reconcile;
unknown impact follows reconciliation. Always reconcile before final qualification and MR opening; keep resource
checks and existing approved contracts. No blanket folder exemption. Expensive CI meta-tests are selected by
actual CI/harness/control dependencies; retain required application/security and hosted path-selected coverage.

For new work, Git commit and path bind committed code and reviewed management inputs; the compact Result records
tested commits, commands, outcomes and relevant runtime inputs. Relevant dirty/untracked changes must be identified.
Extra snapshots/hashes cover only inputs Git does not capture; preserve existing approved bindings and secret-safe
handling. Do not create parallel source inventories or retain whole checkout copies by default. See
[Git-first provenance](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#git-first-provenance).

Follow the [branch workflow](docs/agents/BRANCH-WORKFLOW.md). Before every local batch: fetch, assess the delta,
reconcile unless the documented irrelevant-delta exception applies, and run the environment check
({{PRE_BATCH_ENV_CHECK}}). Batches finish, fix, rerun, then advance. Manual feedback precedes final qualification.
Final validation placement: `{{FINAL_VALIDATION_PLACEMENT}}`; canceled post-merge pipeline satisfies the gate:
`{{CANCELED_PIPELINE_SATISFIES}}`. Integrate through reviewed merge requests to `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` with
`{{MR_TOOL}}`; merge only with owner authorization. Protected branches: {{PROTECTED_BRANCHES}}.

## Closeout and risks

Record temporary-resource ownership at creation in the existing inventory, including exact container/image/volume
IDs. Reuse existing cleanup with one scoped pass and a compact receipt; hold uncertain items and finish the verified
remainder. Unrelated host activity does not block cleanup. On native refusal, report once for owner handling, with no
bypass or repeated approval relays. Preserve required active-run gates. See the closeout reference for details.

Acceptance and cleanup are separate; verify integrated content, unique work and live ownership before removing
anything. After verified terminal closeout, preserve compact acceptance/provenance/failure records and necessary
proof, then purge redundant task-owned local validation logs, traces/videos, reports and scratch by default.
Default durable evidence to committed records and CI artifacts. Local raw files are temporary;
purge closed-task bulk/duplicate downloads, keeping only explicit necessary-proof exceptions with release conditions.
Protect active/dependent/held evidence; verify exact path ownership/containment and no live use; record retained
locations, purged paths/count/bytes, holds and reclaimed space. Do not delete session transcripts/delivery archives
or committed history under this rule. Archive session records before pruning. Compact PROGRESS into dated archives with working links. Every epic closeout requires
a recorded compaction disposition: archive superseded updates, record no changes needed, or explain an
active-binding deferral and its next safe checkpoint. Compaction alone needs no test rerun. Record
performance and other risks in [RISKS](docs/RISKS.md).

## Model-aware effort override — 2026-10-15

Default to Fable 5.1 high for planning and test corrections, including failure
diagnosis and corrective test/fix work; use xhigh selectively for especially
hard work. Default to Opus 5.5 high for implementation, routine test execution
and coordination. If the owner selects Opus 5.5 for planning or test
corrections/diagnosis, use xhigh; routine implementation, test execution and
coordination stay high. This overrides generic role-effort defaults for those
models/phases. Honor owner choices, verify actual settings or report unknown,
and apply only at the next phase/safe boundary without restarting completed work,
interrupting a batch or resetting budgets. Prose does not configure the runtime.


## Fable weekly-limit fallback — owner direction, 2026-10-15

When Fable's weekly allowance is confirmed exhausted, automatically use Opus 5.5
**xhigh for planning and test-failure diagnosis/corrections** until Fable resets.
Opus implementation, ordinary test execution/reporting and coordination remain
**high**. This standing fallback needs no repeat owner approval; honor an explicit
owner override. Record the limit, fallback model/effort and actual setting (or
unknown) in the existing Result/dispatch record. A generic tool error is not
proof of a model limit.

Use supported controls at the next safe boundary, preserving the existing task,
artifacts, checkout, bindings, reviews and attempt count. Do not interrupt a
running validation batch, redo completed work, reset budgets or weaken gates.
If native switching is unavailable or refused, report the blocker once; do not
bypass permissions. Once Fable's allowance reset is verified, return to its normal
model/effort defaults at the next safe phase boundary, without restarting work or
repeatedly probing the allowance. This is model selection only, not scope,
execution-release or budget-extension authority.


Use the shared [validation economy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#validation-economy-and-recurring-failures),
[automatic routine transitions](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#continue-routine-transitions-without-owner-nudges),
[concise messages](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#concise-operational-messages) and
[closeout receipt](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#routine-closeout-receipt).
Preserve explicit owner limits and native permissions; reuse existing records/tools.

Container-produced retained artifacts must be removable by the host operator;
use [artifact ownership](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#container-artifact-ownership)
at creation rather than recurring privileged cleanup.

## Reuse existing coverage in Plans

Inspect relevant existing tests first. In the normal Plan/AC mapping cite test paths
or check IDs as reuse/extend/new, with the gap or distinct boundary for new tests.
Prefer existing fixtures; no cloned suite or new check merely for this Goal. Keep
required regressions, local/hosted/security coverage and current evidence binding.
See [planning coverage reuse](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#reuse-existing-coverage-during-planning).

At handoffs/closeout or when old conversation history dominates context, follow
[coordinator context compaction](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-context-compaction-at-safe-boundaries).
Preserve durable state, session/observer identity and running batches; no per-turn compaction or new monitor.

Use [targeted reruns and attributable coverage](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#targeted-reruns-and-attributable-coverage-default) by default:
rerun failed cases and affected dependencies, reuse unchanged passes, preserve
failed/canceled history, and overlap frozen-source review with independent tests.
Keep required hosted/atomic evidence and final acceptance gates.
